% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 3
% composition-elements: 3/4 metre; dotted half notes (bars 4 and 8); a tika-tika sixteenth group in bars 5 and 7; mf and p signs; a simple two-part round in which the second group enters at bar 5
% composition-listen: Dollar Brand (Abdullah Ibrahim) — "Mannenberg", recorded in Cape Town in June 1974 with Basil Coetzee on tenor saxophone; title track of the album "Mannenberg – 'Is Where It's Happening'" (1974, produced by Rashid Vally)
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Abdullah+Ibrahim+Mannenberg+1974
% composition-bridge: Hear a memorable line become a shared anthem as the ensemble repeats, answers and intensifies it.
% composition-context: "Mannenberg" takes its name from Manenberg, a Cape Flats township built for families forcibly moved under apartheid's Group Areas Act; the 1974 recording became an anthem of the struggle against apartheid. This original 3/4 school round does not reproduce its groove, melody or harmony.
% composition-distance: Original metre, notes and words. The round's 3/4 pentatonic lines share no riff, rhythm, contour, harmony or arrangement with the recording's 4/4 theme or Basil Coetzee's solo.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"4","role":"group 2, waiting to come in","prompt":"Fill group 1's long note, then get ready","choices":["one clap on beat 1","pat on beats 2 and 3","just breathe in"],"replyBars":1}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"7-8","role":"the group singing the last line","prompt":"How loud is 'every voice can bring it home'?","choices":["p","mf","f"],"replyBars":2}
% dossier-function: original two-part classroom round and listening bridge; the class learns one eight-bar melody in unison, then group 2 starts it again when group 1 reaches bar 5, so a single line is carried by many voices before the class listens to "Mannenberg"
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 3/4; C-major pentatonic pitch set C-D-E-G-A; written range C4-D5; bars 1-4 move in half notes and quarters to a dotted-half E4, bars 5-8 in a tika-tika sixteenth group, quarters and two eighths to a dotted-half C4; mf for the first half, p for the second; as a round (%%round 2 every=4) the second group enters at bar 5, so the long-note half always sounds against the moving half over a shared C-F-G-C harmony
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 104 is the target; begin at 84, speaking "o-ver hills and" and "ev-ery voice can" in even sixteenths before singing them. C major is the rehearsal key and the written range C4-D5 sits inside the young-unison C4-D5 range. Teach in steps: echo bars 1-4, then bars 5-8, sing all eight in unison until secure, then split the class with group 2 entering at bar 5; keep the moving half p so the long notes carry. Each group may sing it twice through with Repeat on before stopping in turn
% dossier-accompaniment: The page's waltz style (a light guitar-and-bass three) supports the round; it is a generic school accompaniment, not Cape jazz or marabi, and must not be labelled as either. A teacher at piano or ukulele can play one chord a bar, C-F-G-C, which fits both halves at once; no chord symbols are written, so the melody's grade reading stays on the round itself
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record: "Sing a line and pass it on, / over hills and valleys, over the sea, every voice can bring it home." One syllable per note; stressed syllables fall on beat 1 or on the strong sixteenth; no slurs or ties
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original course composition made for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, grade and form constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community and not modelled on the melody, rhythm or harmony of "Mannenberg"; the recording is a listening bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, words, implied chords and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. "Mannenberg" and its 1974 recording remain the copyright of their owners and are not included
% dossier-respect: Name Abdullah Ibrahim (1934-2026; his official site records his death on 15 June 2026) as the composer and bandleader, and Basil Coetzee, whose tenor solo made the recording famous. Teach the forced removals behind the township's name and the song's life as an anti-apartheid anthem at a level suited to Grade 3, with South African voices and sources. Never call this round, or any repeated school riff, "Cape jazz" or "Mannenberg"
% dossier-review: Grade 3 choir teacher, second score reader and South African jazz educator; all reviews still required
