N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals six eighths with no pickup. Bar 1 E4 half, G4 quarter; bar 2 C5 half, A4 quarter; bar 3 D5 half, G4 quarter; bar 4 E4 dotted half (breath). Bar 5 C4-D4-E4-G4 sixteenths, A4 and G4 quarters; bar 6 A4 quarter, G4-A4 eighths, C5 quarter; bar 7 G4-A4-G4-E4 sixteenths, D4 and G4 quarters; bar 8 C4 dotted half, the tonic. Pitch set C-D-E-G-A, range C4-D5, largest leap a fifth (G4 down to C4, bars 7-8), no syncopation, dots or ties. Form: a rising-and-settling long-note half ending open on mi, answered by a busier half that cadences on do; the halves fit together as a round with entries four bars apart (the page's round-fit score 92%, no downbeat clash). Originality check: the opening E-G-C-A arch and the sixteenth runs were compared with "Mannenberg" (whose theme is a 4/4 riff, not used) and with common school songs and rounds (Row Row Row Your Boat, Frere Jacques, London's Burning, Kookaburra, Lavender's Blue); no match beyond common pentatonic steps.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 3 voices hold the second part against the first at bar 5, whether the sixteenth groups stay clear at 104, and whether the round sounds as good to the ear as its interval score suggests can only be settled by a classroom trial. The title change, the reference's album details (label and release date) and the framing of apartheid history for eight-year-olds need a second reader and a South African jazz educator.
